---
scope: src/tools/scaffold
alignment: unreviewed
description: Tool that creates every AIDE working file, from specs to plans, todo lists and the session record, from its blank template under its canonical name and never over an existing file.
intent: >
  scaffold is the pen agents call to create any AIDE working file from its
  blank template, enforcing naming and placement so an agent can never
  corrupt a folder's layout or destroy work. The default single-spec form
  gives way to the explicit name once research enters a folder, and the
  project root intent spec always takes the explicit name. The session
  record comes only from here, so its placeholder has one source. A plan
  needs an intent spec beside it, so QA always has a contract to verify
  against. scaffold creates; it never overwrites and never deletes.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - The caller picks a desired end state for the folder; the tool resolves which canonical name applies and lands the spec under that name, the explicit form for the project root intent spec and the existing default-or-explicit rule everywhere else. Callers never reason about which form is legal.
    - When research enters a folder holding a default-form spec, the same call renames it to the explicit form and creates the research spec alongside, atomically. A root intent spec an older project left under the default name is likewise moved to the explicit form with its content kept, unless the explicit form already exists, in which case nothing changes.
    - A session record request always lands at the project's one fixed location, whatever folder the caller names, as the empty placeholder with every canonical section heading, nothing under them and no alignment or status. Install and the boot both get the file by calling scaffold, so the placeholder has a single source.
    - A request for any file that already exists, filled or empty, is a no-op, and every action or refusal is reported as one plain-English line the agent can confirm and cite. Replacing a file is the caller's job, done by removing it first through the separate cleanup step; scaffold never overwrites and never deletes.
    - A new intent spec starts unreviewed, and a new plan or todo list starts proposed so it waits for review, while a research spec carries none of these. Scaffold only sets the starting value; changing it later belongs to a separate tool.
    - A plan is created only in a folder that already holds an intent spec; anywhere else the request creates nothing and the refusal names the missing spec, so the agent writes the spec first. A research spec alone does not count, and every other file type is unaffected.
  undesired:
    - A scaffold call that lands the default form beside a research spec, leaves the default and explicit forms side by side, or renames the default when neither research nor a misnamed root spec calls for it.
    - A root intent spec scaffolded under the default name, which the intent-tree lookup does not recognize as the root, so every module's ancestor chain silently loses its top.
    - A scaffold call that resets a filled session record, plan, todo list or spec (or any existing file) to its blank template, or deletes one to make room. The caller meant to create; destruction is never the intended path.
    - A session record created anywhere but the project's one fixed location, produced by install or the boot from a copy of their own instead of scaffold, or a placeholder missing a canonical section heading or carrying content, an alignment or a status. A new intent spec that does not start unreviewed, or a new plan or todo list that does not start proposed, is the same near-miss.
    - A scaffold result returned as a serialized envelope instead of one readable line per action, burying the signal the agent reads to confirm what happened.
    - A plan scaffolded in a folder with no intent spec, or with only a research spec, leaving QA nothing to verify the code against. Equally wrong is the rule leaking to other file types, refusing an intent, research, todo or session request.
---
